1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,999
It's four in the afternoon.

2
00:00:02,039 --> 00:00:04,814
You've answered forty emails, picked a lunch

3
00:00:04,854 --> 00:00:07,192
said yes to a meeting you didn't want...

4
00:00:07,232 --> 00:00:09,882
and now someone asks you one more question.

5
00:00:09,922 --> 00:00:11,883
So… what do you want for dinner?

6
00:00:11,923 --> 00:00:13,135
And your brain just...

7
00:00:13,175 --> 00:00:13,900
stops.

8
00:00:14,950 --> 00:00:16,812
There's a name for that feeling.

9
00:00:16,852 --> 00:00:19,368
Decision fatigue. And it's backed by one of

10
00:00:19,408 --> 00:00:21,627
the most famous studies in psychology

11
00:00:21,667 --> 00:00:23,173
judges who granted parole...

12
00:00:23,213 --> 00:00:25,729
depending on how much time had passed since

13
00:00:25,769 --> 00:00:27,070
their last meal.

14
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:31,061
But here's the part almost nobody mentions.

15
00:00:31,101 --> 00:00:32,656
That study got checked.

16
00:00:32,696 --> 00:00:34,598
So did the theory behind it.

17
00:00:34,638 --> 00:00:37,137
And some of it… didn't survive.

18
00:00:38,187 --> 00:00:39,846
So is decision fatigue real?

19
00:00:39,886 --> 00:00:42,578
The honest answer is stranger than yes or no.

20
00:00:42,618 --> 00:00:44,884
By the end, you'll know which parts to

21
00:00:44,924 --> 00:00:47,277
believe… and what actually helps.

22
00:00:53,127 --> 00:00:55,611
Start with the study that made decision

23
00:00:55,651 --> 00:00:58,135
fatigue famous. In twenty eleven, three

24
00:00:58,175 --> 00:01:01,112
researchers, Shai Danziger, Jonathan Levav and

25
00:01:01,152 --> 00:01:04,024
Liora Avnaim-Pesso, looked at parole hearings

26
00:01:04,064 --> 00:01:05,061
in Israel.

27
00:01:06,111 --> 00:01:09,028
They collected more than a thousand rulings

28
00:01:09,068 --> 00:01:11,784
made by eight experienced judges over ten

29
00:01:11,824 --> 00:01:14,808
months. Each working day was split into three

30
00:01:14,848 --> 00:01:16,690
sessions by two food breaks

31
00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:18,895
a morning snack, and lunch.

32
00:01:19,945 --> 00:01:21,912
Then they plotted one simple thing

33
00:01:21,952 --> 00:01:24,549
the chance that a prisoner was granted parole

34
00:01:24,589 --> 00:01:26,899
depending on where their case fell in the

35
00:01:26,939 --> 00:01:27,748
session.

36
00:01:28,798 --> 00:01:31,648
At the start of a session, about sixty five

37
00:01:31,688 --> 00:01:33,866
percent of requests were granted.

38
00:01:33,906 --> 00:01:36,487
Then, case by case, the line slid down...

39
00:01:36,527 --> 00:01:39,512
until, just before the break, it was close to

40
00:01:39,552 --> 00:01:40,238
zero.

41
00:01:41,288 --> 00:01:43,777
Then the judges took their break.

42
00:01:43,817 --> 00:01:47,072
They ate. And when they came back, the rate

43
00:01:47,112 --> 00:01:50,138
jumped right back up to about sixty five

44
00:01:50,178 --> 00:01:53,900
percent… and started sliding all over again.

45
00:01:54,950 --> 00:01:57,180
Three sessions, three slides, three jumps.

46
00:01:57,220 --> 00:01:59,611
On the graph it looks like the edge of a saw.

47
00:01:59,651 --> 00:02:01,935
And it is very hard to look at that saw and

48
00:02:01,975 --> 00:02:03,622
not feel a little chill.

49
00:02:04,672 --> 00:02:07,033
The authors' reading was that ruling after

50
00:02:07,073 --> 00:02:09,606
ruling wears you down, and a tired mind falls

51
00:02:09,646 --> 00:02:10,978
back on the easy option.

52
00:02:11,018 --> 00:02:13,550
For a parole judge, the easy option is to say

53
00:02:13,590 --> 00:02:15,827
no… and leave things as they are.

54
00:02:16,877 --> 00:02:18,543
The story went everywhere.

55
00:02:18,583 --> 00:02:20,709
Books, talks, productivity blogs.

56
00:02:20,749 --> 00:02:23,465
Daniel Kahneman wrote about it in his best

57
00:02:23,505 --> 00:02:26,089
seller. Your freedom, people said, could

58
00:02:26,129 --> 00:02:28,973
depend on whether the judge had eaten.

59
00:02:30,023 --> 00:02:32,094
And it fit neatly with a theory that

60
00:02:32,134 --> 00:02:34,556
psychologists had been building for over a

61
00:02:34,596 --> 00:02:37,136
decade. A theory that said willpower works a

62
00:02:37,176 --> 00:02:38,640
lot like a battery.

63
00:02:42,290 --> 00:02:44,603
In nineteen ninety eight, Roy Baumeister and

64
00:02:44,643 --> 00:02:46,956
his colleagues ran an experiment that sounds

65
00:02:46,996 --> 00:02:49,255
like a prank. Hungry students walked into a

66
00:02:49,295 --> 00:02:51,555
lab that smelled of freshly baked chocolate

67
00:02:51,595 --> 00:02:52,640
chip cookies.

68
00:02:53,690 --> 00:02:56,136
Some of them were allowed to eat the cookies.

69
00:02:56,176 --> 00:02:58,402
Others had to sit right in front of them...

70
00:02:58,442 --> 00:03:00,173
and eat radishes instead.

71
00:03:01,223 --> 00:03:02,831
Then everyone got a puzzle.

72
00:03:02,871 --> 00:03:05,639
What they weren't told was that it couldn't be

73
00:03:05,679 --> 00:03:08,385
solved. The researchers simply timed how long

74
00:03:08,425 --> 00:03:10,240
each person kept trying.

75
00:03:11,290 --> 00:03:13,601
The radish eaters gave up after about eight

76
00:03:13,641 --> 00:03:15,842
minutes. The cookie eaters kept going for

77
00:03:15,882 --> 00:03:17,107
close to twenty.

78
00:03:18,157 --> 00:03:20,396
Same students, same impossible puzzle.

79
00:03:20,436 --> 00:03:22,975
The only difference was a few minutes spent

80
00:03:23,015 --> 00:03:24,745
not eating the cookies.

81
00:03:25,795 --> 00:03:27,879
The idea they proposed was called ego

82
00:03:27,919 --> 00:03:30,232
depletion. Self-control runs on a limited

83
00:03:30,272 --> 00:03:32,815
resource. Spend some of it resisting cookies

84
00:03:32,855 --> 00:03:35,846
and there's less left for the next hard thing.

85
00:03:36,896 --> 00:03:39,145
Later studies stretched the idea to choosing

86
00:03:39,185 --> 00:03:41,433
itself. People who spent a while making lots

87
00:03:41,473 --> 00:03:43,514
of choices, between products, or college

88
00:03:43,554 --> 00:03:45,802
courses, showed less self-control afterwards

89
00:03:45,842 --> 00:03:48,429
than people who only looked at the options.

90
00:03:49,479 --> 00:03:51,880
Some researchers even suggested the fuel was

91
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:53,489
literally sugar in the blood...

92
00:03:53,529 --> 00:03:55,930
and that a glass of sweet lemonade could top

93
00:03:55,970 --> 00:03:57,429
the battery back up.

94
00:03:58,479 --> 00:04:00,325
Hundreds of studies followed.

95
00:04:00,365 --> 00:04:03,185
By the twenty tens, ego depletion was one of

96
00:04:03,225 --> 00:04:05,461
the best known ideas in psychology.

97
00:04:05,501 --> 00:04:08,452
Which is exactly why people started testing it

98
00:04:08,492 --> 00:04:09,427
properly.

99
00:04:13,077 --> 00:04:15,246
Let's go back to the judges first.

100
00:04:15,286 --> 00:04:18,039
Within months, two other researchers, Keren

101
00:04:18,079 --> 00:04:21,028
Weinshall-Margel and John Shapard, published a

102
00:04:21,068 --> 00:04:23,107
reply in the same journal.

103
00:04:24,157 --> 00:04:26,613
They pointed out that the order of the cases

104
00:04:26,653 --> 00:04:28,996
wasn't random. Prisoners who had no lawyer

105
00:04:29,036 --> 00:04:31,096
tended to be heard last in a session...

106
00:04:31,136 --> 00:04:33,706
and prisoners without lawyers were less likely

107
00:04:33,746 --> 00:04:35,287
to get parole anyway.

108
00:04:36,337 --> 00:04:39,250
The original authors replied that this didn't

109
00:04:39,290 --> 00:04:40,627
explain their result.

110
00:04:40,667 --> 00:04:42,789
But the doubts kept coming.

111
00:04:43,839 --> 00:04:46,666
In twenty sixteen, the psychologist Andreas

112
00:04:46,706 --> 00:04:48,332
Glockner ran simulations.

113
00:04:48,372 --> 00:04:51,066
Granting parole usually takes longer than

114
00:04:51,106 --> 00:04:53,999
refusing it. So picture a judge who sees the

115
00:04:54,039 --> 00:04:57,066
break coming… and doesn't want to start a long

116
00:04:57,106 --> 00:04:57,789
case.

117
00:04:58,839 --> 00:05:00,762
Just that kind of scheduling, with no

118
00:05:00,802 --> 00:05:03,149
tiredness at all, could produce a big part of

119
00:05:03,189 --> 00:05:05,590
the drop. Others noted that a swing from sixty

120
00:05:05,630 --> 00:05:07,871
five percent to almost zero would be one of

121
00:05:07,911 --> 00:05:10,648
the biggest effects psychology has ever seen.

122
00:05:11,698 --> 00:05:14,103
None of this proves the judges weren't tired.

123
00:05:14,143 --> 00:05:16,439
It means the famous number is probably much

124
00:05:16,479 --> 00:05:19,002
bigger than anything fatigue really did.

125
00:05:20,052 --> 00:05:22,198
And the battery? In twenty sixteen, twenty

126
00:05:22,238 --> 00:05:24,489
three labs around the world ran the same ego

127
00:05:24,529 --> 00:05:26,832
depletion experiment, with a method agreed in

128
00:05:26,872 --> 00:05:29,460
advance, and more than two thousand people.

129
00:05:30,510 --> 00:05:31,885
The effect they found...

130
00:05:31,925 --> 00:05:33,433
was close to zero.

131
00:05:34,483 --> 00:05:36,555
Not just small… close to zero.

132
00:05:36,595 --> 00:05:39,795
The original studies had reported effects many

133
00:05:39,835 --> 00:05:41,100
times larger.

134
00:05:42,150 --> 00:05:44,735
In twenty twenty one, an even bigger test

135
00:05:44,775 --> 00:05:47,485
thirty six labs and more than three thousand

136
00:05:47,525 --> 00:05:50,360
people, found an effect so small it was barely

137
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:52,625
different from nothing at all.

138
00:05:53,675 --> 00:05:55,185
And what about the sugar?

139
00:05:55,225 --> 00:05:57,913
Critics showed that the brain's extra use of

140
00:05:57,953 --> 00:06:00,084
glucose during a hard task is tiny.

141
00:06:00,124 --> 00:06:02,874
Far too small to be the fuel gauge the theory

142
00:06:02,914 --> 00:06:03,698
needed.

143
00:06:04,748 --> 00:06:07,817
So… case closed? Decision fatigue is a myth?

144
00:06:07,857 --> 00:06:11,067
Not so fast. Because when researchers left the

145
00:06:11,107 --> 00:06:13,963
lab, and looked at real people doing real

146
00:06:14,003 --> 00:06:16,614
jobs, something kept showing up.

147
00:06:20,264 --> 00:06:22,682
In twenty fourteen, a team in Boston studied

148
00:06:22,722 --> 00:06:25,140
almost twenty two thousand doctor visits for

149
00:06:25,180 --> 00:06:26,872
coughs, colds and sore throats.

150
00:06:26,912 --> 00:06:28,995
Most of these infections are caused by

151
00:06:29,035 --> 00:06:31,787
viruses, so antibiotics usually don't help.

152
00:06:32,837 --> 00:06:34,795
As each clinic session went on, doctors

153
00:06:34,835 --> 00:06:36,999
prescribed antibiotics more and more often.

154
00:06:37,039 --> 00:06:39,356
By the fourth hour, the odds of a prescription

155
00:06:39,396 --> 00:06:42,103
were about a quarter higher than in the first.

156
00:06:43,153 --> 00:06:45,567
Writing the prescription is the quick way to

157
00:06:45,607 --> 00:06:47,518
end a visit with a worried patient.

158
00:06:47,558 --> 00:06:50,362
Saying no, and explaining why, takes effort.

159
00:06:51,412 --> 00:06:53,935
Another team followed more than four thousand

160
00:06:53,975 --> 00:06:56,441
hospital caregivers, with electronic sensors

161
00:06:56,481 --> 00:06:57,751
on the soap dispensers.

162
00:06:57,791 --> 00:07:00,370
Over a twelve hour shift, hand washing fell by

163
00:07:00,410 --> 00:07:02,469
almost nine percentage points.

164
00:07:03,519 --> 00:07:06,064
And financial analysts who had already made

165
00:07:06,104 --> 00:07:08,829
several forecasts that day were more likely to

166
00:07:08,869 --> 00:07:11,595
simply copy the consensus, or repeat their own

167
00:07:11,635 --> 00:07:14,269
earlier number. The low effort answer.

168
00:07:15,319 --> 00:07:16,617
Notice the pattern.

169
00:07:16,657 --> 00:07:19,505
Nobody collapsed. Nobody became unable to

170
00:07:19,545 --> 00:07:20,985
decide. They drifted...

171
00:07:21,025 --> 00:07:22,323
toward the default.

172
00:07:22,363 --> 00:07:25,601
The option that takes the least thinking.

173
00:07:26,651 --> 00:07:29,289
That's why psychologists like Michael Inzlicht

174
00:07:29,329 --> 00:07:30,919
now describe it differently.

175
00:07:30,959 --> 00:07:33,480
Not an empty tank, but a shift in motivation

176
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:36,158
and attention. After a long run of effort, the

177
00:07:36,198 --> 00:07:38,777
mind gets pickier about what deserves effort...

178
00:07:38,817 --> 00:07:41,496
and the easy option starts to look good.

179
00:07:42,546 --> 00:07:45,242
To be fair, these are real world records, not

180
00:07:45,282 --> 00:07:47,856
experiments, so other things could be going

181
00:07:47,896 --> 00:07:50,349
on, like time pressure, or a waiting room

182
00:07:50,389 --> 00:07:52,903
filling up. But the same drift shows up in

183
00:07:52,943 --> 00:07:54,509
very different jobs.

184
00:07:55,559 --> 00:07:57,129
And there's one more twist.

185
00:07:57,169 --> 00:07:59,753
In twenty ten, Veronika Job, Carol Dweck and

186
00:07:59,793 --> 00:08:02,257
Greg Walton found that people who believed

187
00:08:02,297 --> 00:08:04,464
willpower runs out showed the classic

188
00:08:04,504 --> 00:08:07,207
depletion effect… while people who believed it

189
00:08:07,247 --> 00:08:08,968
doesn't, mostly didn't.

190
00:08:10,018 --> 00:08:12,463
That finding needs more replication too.

191
00:08:12,503 --> 00:08:14,949
But it raises an uncomfortable question.

192
00:08:14,989 --> 00:08:17,558
How much of I'm too tired to decide is the

193
00:08:17,598 --> 00:08:19,919
day… and how much is the story we tell

194
00:08:19,959 --> 00:08:21,490
ourselves about it?

195
00:08:25,140 --> 00:08:27,696
So what can you actually do with this?

196
00:08:27,736 --> 00:08:30,291
Here are three ideas, each tied to the

197
00:08:30,331 --> 00:08:33,072
evidence… and each with its limits.

198
00:08:34,122 --> 00:08:36,781
One. Move the decisions that matter.

199
00:08:36,821 --> 00:08:39,854
If the drift shows up after a long run of

200
00:08:39,894 --> 00:08:43,228
choices, don't save your hardest decision for

201
00:08:43,268 --> 00:08:44,652
the end of the day.

202
00:08:44,692 --> 00:08:48,266
Make it while you're fresh, or sleep on it.

203
00:08:49,316 --> 00:08:51,539
Two. Decide once, not every time.

204
00:08:51,579 --> 00:08:54,487
Barack Obama once told a journalist that he

205
00:08:54,527 --> 00:08:57,505
only wore gray or blue suits, because he was

206
00:08:57,545 --> 00:08:59,562
trying to pare down decisions.

207
00:08:59,602 --> 00:09:01,666
You don't need a uniform.

208
00:09:02,716 --> 00:09:05,092
But turning a repeated choice into a simple

209
00:09:05,132 --> 00:09:07,507
if-then plan, like, if it's Monday, I go to

210
00:09:07,547 --> 00:09:10,092
the gym straight after work, has helped people

211
00:09:10,132 --> 00:09:12,227
follow through across nearly a hundred

212
00:09:12,267 --> 00:09:13,066
studies.

213
00:09:14,116 --> 00:09:16,466
Fewer decisions made on the spot means fewer

214
00:09:16,506 --> 00:09:17,780
chances to drift.

215
00:09:18,830 --> 00:09:21,419
Three. Take real breaks, and watch out for the

216
00:09:21,459 --> 00:09:24,048
default. In the hospital study, caregivers who

217
00:09:24,088 --> 00:09:26,505
had longer rest between shifts washed their

218
00:09:26,545 --> 00:09:29,010
hands more often on their next shift.

219
00:09:30,060 --> 00:09:32,952
And when you catch yourself reaching for the

220
00:09:32,992 --> 00:09:35,417
easy answer at the end of a long day...

221
00:09:35,457 --> 00:09:37,216
treat it as a cue to pause.

222
00:09:37,256 --> 00:09:40,471
Not as proof that the easy answer is right.

223
00:09:41,521 --> 00:09:43,064
And maybe a gentle fourth one.

224
00:09:43,104 --> 00:09:44,859
Be careful with the battery story.

225
00:09:44,899 --> 00:09:47,287
Telling yourself you're out of willpower might

226
00:09:47,327 --> 00:09:49,471
make it truer than it needs to be.

227
00:09:50,521 --> 00:09:52,888
So, back to four in the afternoon, and that

228
00:09:52,928 --> 00:09:55,406
dinner question. Your brain didn't run out of

229
00:09:55,446 --> 00:09:57,589
fuel. After a long day of choices, it's

230
00:09:57,629 --> 00:09:59,771
leaning toward whatever takes the least

231
00:09:59,811 --> 00:10:02,288
effort… and now you know to notice it.

232
00:10:03,338 --> 00:10:05,518
So, decision fatigue isn't a myth.

233
00:10:05,558 --> 00:10:08,195
It's just smaller, and stranger, than the

234
00:10:08,235 --> 00:10:10,153
story about the hungry judges.

235
00:10:10,193 --> 00:10:13,026
Those judges probably weren't ruled by their

236
00:10:13,066 --> 00:10:15,964
lunch. But you, at four in the afternoon, may

237
00:10:16,004 --> 00:10:18,706
really be a little more likely to take the

238
00:10:18,746 --> 00:10:19,945
easy way out.

239
00:10:20,995 --> 00:10:22,192
So tell me in the comments.

240
00:10:22,232 --> 00:10:24,208
What's the decision you always put off until

241
00:10:24,248 --> 00:10:25,491
you're too tired to make it?

242
00:10:25,531 --> 00:10:27,324
And if you like your psychology with the

243
00:10:27,364 --> 00:10:28,240
receipts, subscribe.

244
00:10:28,280 --> 00:10:30,005
There's a new page every week.
